<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Mixed Workload — Spike (recovery test)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Spike test for the mixed-workload boundary. Surge all four endpoints
from 70% baseline to 200% spike simultaneously, hold 3 min, then drop
back to 70% to observe whether the system recovers cleanly when load
returns to baseline across the full traffic mix.

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ BOUNDARY (from mixed-workload stress run): 182 total VUs             ║
║                                                                      ║
║   Composition (10:1:1:2 ratio):                                      ║
║                                                                      ║
║   Endpoint        Boundary  Baseline 70%  Spike 200%                 ║
║   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────                 ║
║   Account read   130        91            260                        ║
║   Deposit         13         9             26                        ║
║   Withdraw        13         9             26                        ║
║   Transfer        26        18             52                        ║
║   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────                 ║
║   Total          182        127           364                        ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Sized at 200% (not 150%) on the same rationale as the per-endpoint   ║
║ spike plans: 150% (273) would just replay the load test's just-over  ║
║ zone, not test true surge. 200% (364) is well into the catastrophic  ║
║ regime — the stress run showed 17% error rate at 280 total VUs and   ║
║ rising. At 364 VUs every write endpoint is multiple times past its   ║
║ isolated boundary; the spike phase EXPECTS heavy errors. The         ║
║ question is whether the system DEGRADES GRACEFULLY (returns proper   ║
║ 5xx, keeps the event loop responsive) and RECOVERS to baseline       ║
║ once the spike ends.                                                 ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Recovery is the headline metric. The transfer write path is the      ║
║ slowest to release Postgres row locks; if those leak past the        ║
║ spike, the recovery phase will show elevated p95 / errors at the     ║
║ same 127 VU total that ran clean before the spike.                   ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Load-generator memory: 364 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈ 364 MB   ║
║ peak. Default JMeter heap (1 GB) is plenty.                          ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Spike ramp rate: +237 total threads over 5s ≈ 47 threads/s. JMeter   ║
║ handles this comfortably; no ramp jitter risk at this scale.         ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

Shape (4 concurrent Ultimate Thread Groups, synchronized, 13:30 total):

  ┌──────────────┬─────────────┬───────┬─────────────────────────┐
  │ Window       │ Total VUs   │ Hold  │ Maps to spec            │
  ├──────────────┼─────────────┼───────┼─────────────────────────┤
  │ 0–5 min      │ 127         │  5 m  │ 70%  (warm-up)          │
  │ 5–8 min      │ 364         │  3 m  │ 200% (the SPIKE)        │
  │ 8–13:30 min  │ 127         │  5 m  │ 70%  (recovery)         │
  └──────────────┴─────────────┴───────┴─────────────────────────┘

Pass criteria:
  - During warm-up and recovery: per-endpoint p95 and error rate
    matching the load-plan SLAs:
      Read   :  p95 &lt; 200ms,  errors &lt; 1%
      Deposit:  p95 &lt; 5000ms, errors &lt; 5%
      Withdraw: p95 &lt; 5000ms, errors &lt; 5%
      Transfer: p95 &lt; 5000ms, errors &lt; 10%
  - During the spike: elevated errors and latency are acceptable —
    measure GRACEFUL DEGRADATION (proper 5xx, no event-loop stall)
    and FULL RECOVERY to baseline SLA once the spike ends.

A spike that leaves the system degraded after VUs drop back to 127
is a failure even if the spike phase itself returned proper 5xx codes.

Response Assertions (per TG):
  - Read     : exactly 200
  - Deposit  : ^(2\d\d|422)$
  - Withdraw : ^(2\d\d|422)$
  - Transfer : ^(2\d\d|422)$

Think time: Gaussian Random Timer on each TG (mean 3s, σ 1s).

Amounts:
  - Deposit:  $100.00
  - Withdraw: $50.25  (non-round, &lt;$3k)
  - Transfer: $0.10

⚠ Weekend caveat: transfer TG bypasses lock+update+insert on Sat/Sun
UTC. Run on a weekday for meaningful recovery measurement.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/spike/mixed-workload.spike.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/mixed-workload-spike.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/mixed-workload-spike-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:spike:mixed

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
